{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 47, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a powerful figure in the 12th century known for his military prowess and political acumen. He ruled the County of Tripoli from 1101 to 1141 effectively consolidating its position as a significant power in the Levant. His reign marked a period of prosperity and expansion for the county although also facing constant threats from neighboring entities.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension* Raymond was the son of Raymond I Count of Tripoli and William of Poitou. He inherited the county upon his father’s death in 1101. He immediately faced challenges asserting his authority and defending Tripoli from various enemies. He skillfully navigated complex alliances often aligning himself with the Byzantine Empire and other regional powers to maintain stability. Raymond's early rule was characterized by military campaigns and diplomatic efforts to secure Tripoli’s borders. He fostered economic growth through trade and agriculture establishing Tripoli as a vital trading hub connecting Europe and the East.\n\nRaymond III’s reign witnessed significant military achievements. He successfully repelled numerous attacks from Muslim forces and expanded the county’s territory. He participated in several crusades demonstrating his commitment to the Holy Land and securing further resources and prestige for Tripoli. He also engaged in conflicts with the Normans whose growing power threatened Tripoli's autonomy. Raymond’s military strategies and leadership were instrumental in maintaining Tripoli’s independence for a considerable period.\n\n*Political and Economic Policies* Raymond's governance was marked by a blend of military strength and shrewd political maneuvering. He established a strong administrative system promoting justice and order within the county. He encouraged trade and attracted merchants from various regions contributing to Tripoli’s economic prosperity. He also oversaw the development of infrastructure including roads and fortifications enhancing the county’s connectivity and defense capabilities. His policies fostered a relatively stable environment that attracted skilled artisans and merchants further boosting the economy. He skillfully balanced relations with various powers ensuring Tripoli’s continued autonomy.\n\n*Legacy and Death* Raymond III Count of Tripoli died in 1141. His death marked the end of a significant era in Tripoli’s history. While his reign was relatively short he left a lasting legacy of military strength economic prosperity and political stability. He is remembered as a capable ruler who successfully defended Tripoli from numerous threats and fostered its growth as a major regional power. His legacy continues to be studied by historians who analyze his contributions to the history of the Levant and the Crusades. Tripoli under his rule became a crucial link in the medieval world connecting Europe Asia and North Africa. His successors faced challenges continuing to manage the county’s complex political landscape and military defense.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.14342188835144043}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -4.5060096454108134e-05}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁was", "logprob": -0.0798519179224968}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.04111293703317642}, {"token": "▁powerful", "logprob": -0.5501455068588257}, {"token": "▁figure", "logprob": -1.6038830280303955}, {"token": "▁in", "logprob": -0.024912608787417412}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.003749603871256113}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.6708590984344482}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.0015584002248942852}, {"token": "th", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁century", "logprob": -0.04701812192797661}, {"token": "▁known", "logprob": -